==Old English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{term/t|gem-pro|*bindaną}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{term/t|ine-pro|*bʰendʰ-||bind}}.  Cognate with Old Saxon {{term|bindan|lang=osx}} (Low German {{term|binnen|lang=nds}}), Old Dutch {{term|bindan|lang=odt}} (Dutch {{term|binden|lang=nl}}), Old High German {{term|bintan|lang=goh}} (German {{term|binden|lang=de}}), Old Norse {{term|binda|lang=non}} (Danish {{term|binde|lang=da}}, Swedish {{term|binda|lang=sv}}), Gothic {{term|𐌱𐌹𐌽𐌳𐌰𐌽|tr=bindan|lang=got}}.  The Indo-European root was also the source of Avestan ''bendayaiti'' and Greek {{l/grc|πεῖσμα}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈbindɑn/|lang=ang}}

===Verb===
{{ang-verb|type=strong|class=III}}

# to [[bind]], [[constrain]]
# to [[fasten]] or [[join]]

====Conjugation====
{{ang-conj-strong-3a|b|nd}}

====Descendants====
* Middle English: {{l|enm|binden}}
** English: [[bind]]

